"use strict";
// Original guide prose; dated references and kit-based suggestions are identified per page.
const GUIDES = {
	beginner: {
		group: "start",
		title: "Beginner Guide",
		sub: "A first-week plan for a stable party, useful pulls and fewer wasted upgrades.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Build one dependable four-savior party before spreading upgrades. Progress Operation alongside the story; train your core through Journey; preserve scarce skill materials until you know which abilities matter. Battles use four slots, Journey lasts 46 turns, and attribute advantage adds 5% base damage. Check separate PvE and PvP ratings before investing. The stored PvP usage snapshot is not live and is not a beginner shopping list.</p>\n<h2>What kind of game is this?</h2><p>Star Savior alternates between turn-based battles and training runs. Your combat party needs enough damage to finish an encounter and enough protection to keep that damage active. Journey develops permanent combat-stat contributions, while equipment, skill levels and resonance add separate layers. A high rarity alone does not solve an enemy mechanic. Start with the <a href="#/tiers">mode tier list</a>, then read the actual kit of each candidate. The <a href="#/saviors">roster</a> also shows stored PvP usage; frequent competitive use can reflect advanced investment and says little about what a fresh account can afford.</p>\n<h2>What should I do on day one?</h2><ol><li>Advance Operation and read the connected main-story chapters; collect the mission rewards you actually unlock.</li><li>Choose four owned saviors covering a protected attacker, recovery and the encounter\'s utility requirement.</li><li>Check the chosen attacker\'s skills, then improve the support ability that keeps that attacker working.</li><li>Complete an accessible Journey and compare the resulting stats with your earlier archive.</li><li>Spend available Willpower on your current progression bottleneck; review event rewards before their displayed expiry.</li></ol><p>For the rest of the week, repeat this loop and change one weak link at a time. A failed fight tells you whether you lack survival, damage, debuff reliability or correct targeting. The <a href="#/teams">team examples</a> are starting points; missing a suggested unit is a reason to substitute the same job, not to abandon your account.</p>\n<h2>Which resources should I protect?</h2><p>Use banner-specific tickets where their in-game description allows them, and reserve flexible Stellagems until you have inspected the active banner and its guarantee. Spend progression materials on the four units doing your current clears. Save rare skill cubes for upgrades that unlock a real effect or improve a frequently used action. Journey stamina is an internal run resource; it is distinct from the account\'s Willpower. Check the <a href="#/guide/systems-combat">combat basics guide</a> for systems and the equipment guide for slot restrictions before buying or upgrading a replacement.</p><div class="callout warn">Opinions differ on how deep to upgrade. Stopping early and going deep are both valid for some accounts, so neither is a universal rule. Use the exact stored skill upgrade and your bottleneck to decide.</div>\n<h2>Who should I pull and build first?</h2><p>Fill the missing job first. Emily offers protection for an attacker, Waltz Asherah offers recovery and cleansing, and Bunny Charlotte offers focused Sun damage with party ATK support. Lacy brings self-sustain rather than party healing. These jobs are different even when their PvE letters are similar. Read the <a href="#/guide/pull-guide">first-pulls guide</a> before spending: a unit that needs a particular attribute, trigger or teammate can be slower to assemble than an independent option. Current banner availability, free selectors and reroll rewards have not been verified in a live account, so no advertised starting package is guaranteed here.</p>\n<h2>Which mistakes slow the first week?</h2><p>Spreading scarce upgrades across the entire roster leaves your main party unable to clear its next gate. Copying an old five-name team as five deployed saviors is another trap: combat has four slots, and older suggestions often append a Break Skill candidate. Do not confuse that selection with a fifth teammate. Avoid assuming a Supporter always heals or a Defender always taunts; read the skill targets. Finally, do not replace a useful early unit solely because of rarity. Keep it until an owned alternative solves the same problem with a clearer benefit at your available investment.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Must I reroll?</summary><p>No. The current account-reset flow, time cost and starting rewards are unconfirmed here. A functioning party is enough to begin.</p></details><details><summary>Do I need a mono-attribute party?</summary><p>Only when the kit or encounter rewards it enough to justify the missing utility.</p></details><details><summary>Does Lacy heal my team?</summary><p>Her listed response engine heals herself. Bring party recovery separately when needed.</p></details><details><summary>Should I follow PvP usage?</summary><p>Use it as a dated competitive clue; it does not measure beginner accessibility.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/guide/pull-guide">First pulls</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/team-building">Team building</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/journey">Journey planning</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"team-building": {
		group: "start",
		title: "Team Building Guide",
		sub: "Choose four compatible jobs, a safe formation and a separate Break Skill.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Start with four saviors who can survive long enough for one attacker to finish the fight. Cover protection and recovery, then spend the flexible slot on damage, control or breaking. Positions are not guaranteed targeting. Sun beats Star, Star beats Moon, and Moon beats Sun; Order and Chaos counter each other. Select the Break Skill separately from the four deployed members.</p><h2>How do the four slots and positions work?</h2><p>A useful first draft is a frontline protector, a recovery unit, a main attacker and a flexible utility slot. These are jobs, not rigid class requirements: an individual kit can cover more than one. Enemy targeting rules decide who is attacked; many ordinary target patterns favor the front row, but area and specially targeted attacks can bypass your plan. A Defender without Taunt does not guarantee every hit. Read the <a href="#/guide/systems-turns">Party Position and Targeting rules</a>, then check the actual boss skills before relying on formation alone.</p><h2>Which roles and attributes should I combine?</h2><p>Main DPS needs uptime; a healer needs living allies to recover; a debuffer needs effects that the target can receive. Fit those requirements before chasing a uniform attribute. Sun → Star → Moon → Sun and Order ↔ Chaos describe advantage, but team bonuses depend on skill wording. An off-attribute protector can be better than an incompatible fourth attacker. Conversely, Sun turns can matter to Bunny Charlotte’s resource loop and Star allies can benefit from Lyn’s support branch. Use the <a href="#/builder">builder</a> to compare owned units, then read each dependency note.</p><h2>How should I choose a Break Skill?</h2><p>The equipped Break Skill is a separate selection; it does not add a fifth savior to battle. Match its effects to the encounter and the window your team can exploit. A damage-focused skill is wasted if you cannot finish the enemy during the opening, while utility may solve a specific incoming threat. The older <a href="#/teams">team database</a> sometimes lists an appended Break Skill candidate; its fifth name is not a legal deployed slot. For exact damage and Nova interactions, consult the <a href="#/guide/systems-break">Break guide</a> instead of treating the chosen candidate’s ordinary kit as the Break Skill.</p><h2>Five worked team examples</h2><p>These are kit-based starting points, not measured best-in-slot lineups. Every example has exactly four members. Substitute an owned savior by the job and conditions described, not merely by the letter of her tier. After a loss, identify whether the failure happened before the first heal, during a control lockout, or because damage arrived too slowly.</p><h3>Protected Sun carry</h3><p><a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily · Candle Square</a> · <a href="#/savior/dress-stranis">Asherah · Waltz of Starlight</a> · <a href="#/savior/bunny-knight">Charlotte · Heart of Monastir</a> · <a href="#/savior/independent-dragon">Lacy · Wanderer</a>.</p><p>Emily protects the focused attacker; Waltz Asherah supplies recovery; Bunny Charlotte buffs ATK and attacks; Lacy adds pressure and personal sustain. Watch targeting: Lacy gains less from her response loop when enemies spend their actions on someone else. This mixed party prioritizes a workable protected carry over perfect attribute purity. Against waves, replace one focused attacker with an owned area option.</p><h3>Sun pressure with control</h3><p><a href="#/savior/bunny-knight">Charlotte · Heart of Monastir</a> · <a href="#/savior/counterside-hilde">Hilde · Fenrir Squad</a> · <a href="#/savior/dress-stranis">Asherah · Waltz of Starlight</a> · <a href="#/savior/saintess-demon">Muriel · Monolith Order</a>.</p><p>Hilde supplies frontline protection, Asherah recovery, Charlotte focused damage and Muriel pressure through her debuff-oriented kit. Inspect the enemy immunity list before counting on Muriel. This arrangement covers protection and healing without asking Charlotte to clear the whole wave alone. If the enemy removes your advantages faster than you establish them, change the utility slot rather than copying the team unchanged.</p><h3>Moon focused damage</h3><p><a href="#/savior/bunny-spear">Claire · Flawless Blue Rose</a> · <a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily · Candle Square</a> · <a href="#/savior/dress-stranis">Asherah · Waltz of Starlight</a> · <a href="#/savior/recon-knuckle">Roberta · Orbital Search Team</a>.</p><p>Claire handles the priority target, Emily protects her, Waltz Asherah recovers the party and Seira provides tempo utility. Set the utility actions before Claire’s valuable damage window when speed and cooldowns permit it. Emily is a deliberate off-attribute protector here; do not claim that all four units receive Moon-only kit benefits. Critical reliability and surviving the opener matter more than a nominal mono-attribute label.</p><h3>Star branch planning</h3><p><a href="#/savior/eastern-swordmaster">Lyn · Wind Moon Sect</a> · <a href="#/savior/kingdom-knight">Charlotte · Monastir Knights</a> · <a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily · Candle Square</a> · <a href="#/savior/magician-druid">Dana · Canopus Rangers</a>.</p><p>Lyn offers Star pressure and branch-dependent support, Charlotte covers defensive debuffs, Emily protects a chosen attacker and Dana adds area damage. This is an offensive example with limited recovery, so bring it only when barriers and the encounter duration are sufficient. For sustained damage, replace a damage slot with a real healer. Choose Lyn’s area-support or focused branch deliberately instead of treating her skill order as cosmetic.</p><h3>Sustain around an oath carry</h3><p><a href="#/savior/wedding-shielder">Carmen · Eternal Promise</a> · <a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily · Candle Square</a> · <a href="#/savior/bunny-spear">Claire · Flawless Blue Rose</a> · <a href="#/savior/dress-stranis">Asherah · Waltz of Starlight</a>.</p><p>Carmen protects the starting highest-ATK ally, Emily adds targeted protection, Claire supplies focused damage and Asherah supplies party recovery. Check that Claire actually receives the oath after equipment changes. Double protection trades damage for survival; it is a response to a dangerous encounter, not an automatic fastest-clear composition. If the party survives comfortably but times out, exchange one protector for compatible pressure.</p><h2>What breaks an otherwise strong party?</h2><p>Overlapping buffs, conflicting targeting requirements and incompatible resource engines can make individually strong saviors work poorly together. A Taunt can divert hits from a reactive attacker; an ATK change can move Carmen’s starting oath to the wrong ally; a boss immunity can disable your debuffer’s main job. Test one change at a time. A party that dies before the opener needs survival or tempo, while a party that survives but times out needs more effective damage. Do not infer either problem from Combat Power alone.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Can I deploy five saviors?</summary><p>No. The fifth entry in some older suggestions represents the separate Break Skill choice.</p></details><details><summary>Must every party contain a Defender?</summary><p>No, but replacing protection requires a concrete plan for incoming damage.</p></details><details><summary>Is mono-attribute always best?</summary><p>No. Use it when its kit benefits outweigh the lost utility.</p></details><details><summary>Are these teams benchmarked?</summary><p>No. They explain kit compatibility and limitations; adjust for your roster and encounter.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/teams">Team suggestions</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-combat">Combat rules</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-break">Break and Nova</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	journey: {
		group: "start",
		title: "Best Journey Guide",
		sub: "Plan the 46 turns, convert training stats and build better archives.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Choose a difficulty you can finish, then train the stats your savior actually scales with. Every scenario has 46 turns; stat caps are 500, 750 and 1,250. Hard recommends Resonance 8 and gives 250% PP scaling. Two equipped Stellar Archives feed the succession loop. An S score starts at 9,000; survival, condition and useful potentials matter more than chasing the next letter on an unfinished run.</p><h2>Which difficulty should I choose?</h2><table><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Stat cap</th><th>PP scale</th><th>Recommended Resonance</th></tr><tr><td>Easy</td><td>500</td><td>100%</td><td>None listed</td></tr><tr><td>Normal</td><td>750</td><td>150%</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>Hard</td><td>1,250</td><td>250%</td><td>8</td></tr></table><p>Hard raises the potential ceiling, but a recommendation is not a promise that a particular build can clear. Review your leader’s scaling, available Arcana levels and archives before increasing difficulty. A completed easier run can give you a more useful next archive than an ambitious failure. The PP multiplier affects the run’s potential economy; it does not multiply every combat stat by the same percentage. Keep these separate when comparing two outcomes.</p><h2>How do the 46 turns unfold?</h2><p>The early pattern generally gives six ordinary turns before a boss. Town transitions arrive on turns 15 and 30, with archive companions joining in slot order at those transitions. The closing stretch shortens the preparation windows: bosses occur on turns 41 and 45, and turn 46 ends the run. Spend an ordinary turn to address a known weakness before the next hurdle rather than discovering it in battle. The <a href="data/journey-events.json">local Journey-event choices</a> show requirements and possible rewards; random event availability and success are not guaranteed.</p><h2>How do training stats become combat stats?</h2><table><tr><th>Journey stat</th><th>Combat contribution before role factor</th></tr><tr><td>Strength</td><td>ATK ×1</td></tr><tr><td>Vitality</td><td>HP ×6</td></tr><tr><td>Endurance</td><td>DEF ×0.5</td></tr><tr><td>Protection</td><td>Effect RES ×0.0004</td></tr><tr><td>Focus</td><td>Effect Hit ×0.0004</td></tr></table><p><b>Worked example:</b> an Assassin with 250 Strength receives 250 × 1 × 1.2 = <b>300 ATK</b> from that stat. With 80 Vitality, the HP contribution is 80 × 6 × 0.9 = <b>432 HP</b>. These are contributions, not the full final build. Strikers and Rangers use Strength ×1.1 and Endurance ×0.9. Casters and Assassins use Strength ×1.2, Vitality ×0.9 and Endurance ×0.9. Defenders and Supporters use Strength ×0.8 and Vitality/Endurance ×1.1. Protection and Focus are stored as fractions: 25 ×0.0004 is 0.01, or one percentage point.</p><h2>When should I rest?</h2><p>Keep condition and stamina high enough for the next boss, not merely the next training click. Condition 5 gives +10% battle SPD; condition 1 gives −10%. Stamina 80 falls in the +20% HP band, while stamina 95 falls in the +30% band; at 20 the penalty is −20%. Poor condition and low stamina also make training less reliable. Rest or choose a recovery-oriented action before this compounds into failed training and a weak boss entry. Event choices can trade stamina or coins for uncertain rewards, so reserve resources for the approaching hurdle.</p><div class="callout warn">The DB labels overlap at stamina 50 and 90. The examples avoid those boundaries; inspect the in-game buff at the exact cutoff. Its overview and detailed training tables also give different sample costs and gains. The table below explicitly follows the detailed base-gain rows.</div><h2>What do training levels change?</h2><table><tr><th>Training</th><th>Level 1 success</th><th>Level 10 success</th></tr><tr><td>Strength</td><td>+13 STR, +5 END, +5 PP</td><td>+25 STR, +10 END, +14 PP</td></tr><tr><td>Vitality</td><td>+13 VIT, +5 END, +5 PP</td><td>+25 VIT, +10 END, +14 PP</td></tr><tr><td>Endurance</td><td>+10 END, +4 STR/VIT, +6 PP</td><td>+20 END, +7 STR/VIT, +19 PP</td></tr><tr><td>Focus</td><td>+9 FOC, +2 STR, net +3 stamina</td><td>+19 FOC, +4 STR, net +3 stamina</td></tr><tr><td>Protection</td><td>+9 PRT, +2 VIT, net +3 stamina</td><td>+19 PRT, +4 VIT, net +3 stamina</td></tr></table><p>Failure gives only +5 to the primary stat in this table, so a successful weaker session can beat a high-risk nominal upgrade. Strength and Vitality training offer direct specialization; Endurance mixes defense with secondary physical stats. Focus and Protection can recover stamina on success but should still serve a useful build goal. Arcana, town bonuses and sensory effects modify the base outcome. Compare the displayed session reward and failure chance in your run rather than treating these unmodified rows as a guaranteed final gain.</p><h2>Which Arcana and archives should I bring?</h2><p>Choose the stat tag you expect to train most, then compare training effectiveness, starting bond and the card’s unique requirement at its actual level. A character-name match with a party member adds 5% Training Effect. Strength options include No Pain, No Gain and The Indomitable Masterpiece; Vitality options include A Knight’s Oath and Young Lady of Stranis. Equip two Stellar Archives whose saviors differ from the leader. Succession always activates stat blessings, while standard blessings are probabilistic. The finished run creates another archive, letting later runs build on the earlier result.</p><h2>Which scores and mistakes should I watch?</h2><p>A begins at 8,600, S at 9,000, SS at 11,800 and SSS at 14,600. Treat these as thresholds rather than a reason to sacrifice the build’s useful stats. Common errors are choosing a card by rarity alone, neglecting condition until the boss, and purchasing potentials that do not interact with the leader’s actual skills. Check the <a href="#/potentials">potential descriptions</a> and <a href="#/guide/arcana-guide">Arcana guide</a>. A higher grade is helpful, but compare the combat-stat mix and retained potential package before deciding which archive to carry forward.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Does Hard have more turns?</summary><p>No. All three scenarios use 46 turns.</p></details><details><summary>Can I equip the leader’s own archive?</summary><p>No. Both equipped archives must be from other saviors.</p></details><details><summary>Is Sensory Training guaranteed by matching tags?</summary><p>No. A matching tag enables the chance roll.</p></details><details><summary>Why did the outcome differ from the table?</summary><p>The table is the DB’s detailed base success table. Arcana, level effects, condition, failure and events change the run.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/guide/arcana-guide">Arcana selection</a>.</li><li><a href="data/journey-events.json">Journey events</a>.</li><li><a href="#/potentials">Potentials</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	meta: {
		group: "start",
		title: "Latest meta snapshot",
		sub: "Separate published mode ratings, stored usage and what is still unknown.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Use the current stored ratings as a shortlist, not proof of a new patch winner. PvE and PvP ratings are separate, usage is a stored snapshot, and no verified before-and-after patch comparison is available. Latest-patch tier movement is unknown. Build a needed role first, then inspect the Savior’s kit, partners and investment cost.</p><h2>What changed this patch?</h2><p>A reliable balance-patch verdict needs the patch notes and comparable before-and-after evidence. This snapshot has neither a verified full current balance changelog nor a historical rating series. We therefore cannot say who rose because of a buff, who fell because of a nerf, or whether a new arrival changed an entire mode. The ratings are a snapshot, not a confirmed game update.</p><p>The mechanics database mentions a 12/11/2026 Star Link slot-expansion item, but that is not evidence of a character balance change. Keep the distinction when comparing guides: a recently edited article can still contain older opinions or a different server context.</p><h2>Tier movement is unverified; mode differences are real</h2><p>There is no defensible movement table without previous ratings under the same method. The table below instead compares the published PvE and PvP fields currently stored here. A difference between columns is a mode recommendation, not a rise or fall over time. NEW marks a provisional entry without a verified published mode grade; it is not a hidden low tier.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Savior</th><th>Stored PvE</th><th>Stored PvP</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily (Candle Square)</a></td><td>S+</td><td>S</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/maid-lantern">Carnelia (Wanderer)</a></td><td>S</td><td>S+</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/west-beamsword">Ceres (Western Liberators)</a></td><td>A+</td><td>S+</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/detective-professor">Professor M (Lampadis)</a></td><td>S+</td><td>S+</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/wedding-shielder">Carmen (Eternal Promise)</a></td><td>S+</td><td>S+</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/summer-oracle">Luna (White Pearl Trap)</a></td><td>A+</td><td>S+</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>PvP participation: a clue, not a win rate</h2><p>The table is generated from the local savior list. It records participation values without a collection date, sample size or confirmed arena split. The table is a snapshot and does not update while you play. A heavily used Savior may be common because of availability, a current strategy or account investment; none of those explanations is proven by the participation number alone.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Savior</th><th>Stored usage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="#/savior/summer-oracle">Luna (White Pearl Trap)</a></td><td>87%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/wedding-shielder">Carmen (Eternal Promise)</a></td><td>85%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/independent-vagabond">Fei (Wanderer)</a></td><td>53%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/maid-lantern">Carnelia (Wanderer)</a></td><td>48%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/stranisearl-roseblade">Amora (House Stranis)</a></td><td>43%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/west-beamsword">Ceres (Western Liberators)</a></td><td>17%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/counterside-hilde">Hilde (Fenrir Squad)</a></td><td>16%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily (Candle Square)</a></td><td>13%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Read high-participation kits to learn what your team may face. Then inspect the actual opponent and current Rotation Ban list. Do not treat a high usage percentage as a required pull or a measured chance of winning. A kit-based counter with suitable gear can matter more than another popular unit whose role overlaps your existing roster.</p><h2>What should you build now?</h2><p>If you are still progressing through PvE, finish one coherent four-slot team before widening the roster. Choose a carry whose scaling you can support, a durable slot that solves incoming damage, and the recovery or buffs required by that encounter. <a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily (Candle Square)</a> and <a href="#/savior/detective-professor">Professor M (Lampadis)</a> have strong stored PvE ratings, but read their actual partner and gear needs before spending scarce upgrades. A rating is the start of that decision.</p><p>For PvP, prepare alternatives in the same job because draft and Rotation Ban can disrupt one fixed lineup. <a href="#/savior/summer-oracle">Luna (White Pearl Trap)</a> and <a href="#/savior/wedding-shielder">Carmen (Eternal Promise)</a> lead this usage snapshot, yet that does not establish their current ban stages. For either mode, prefer an upgrade that fixes your observed failure: speed order, healing access, reliable debuffs or damage during Break. Keep new or provisional entries on a review list until their stored kit and published evidence justify the investment.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Who moved up in the latest patch?</summary><p>Unconfirmed. This dataset has no verified comparable historical rating series.</p></details><details><summary>Why do two tier sites disagree?</summary><p>Their mode priorities, evidence dates and editorial methods differ. A disagreement is not proof of a patch change.</p></details><details><summary>Does NEW mean weak?</summary><p>No. It marks an entry without a verified published mode grade here.</p></details><details><summary>Is the usage table live?</summary><p>No. It is a stored snapshot with an unknown collection window.</p></details><details><summary>Should I pull every S+ Savior?</summary><p>No. Check your missing role, available partners, gear and the current banner details first.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/tiers">Full mode tier lists</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/pull-guide">First pulls</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-pvp">PvP rules and team ideas</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	stats: {
		group: "start",
		title: "Stats explained",
		sub: "Read a skill multiplier and spend upgrades on the stat it uses.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Read the scaling line before choosing an upgrade. HP keeps a Savior alive; ATK and sometimes HP or DEF power skills. SPD sets Action gauge speed. CRIT Rate and CRIT DMG solve different problems. Effect Hit is for debuffs, while attack accuracy handles evasion. A bigger Battle Power number does not explain which of these your team lacks.</p><h2>The core stats and their jobs</h2><p>A skill’s scaling line tells you which stats feed its damage, healing or Barrier. “ATK scaling” means the effect grows with the user’s ATK; mixed scaling adds more than one stat contribution. HP and DEF can therefore improve offense on the right Savior as well as keeping them alive. Do not build every attacker the same way just because its class looks offensive.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Stat</th><th>What to check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>HP</td><td>Survival and any Max HP scaling in the skill</td></tr><tr><td>ATK</td><td>ATK-based damage, healing or Barriers</td></tr><tr><td>DEF</td><td>Incoming normal damage mitigation and any DEF scaling</td></tr><tr><td>SPD</td><td>Time until the next turn, together with current Action gauge</td></tr><tr><td>CRIT Rate / CRIT DMG</td><td>Chance of a critical hit / its added damage contribution</td></tr><tr><td>Effect Hit / Effect RES</td><td>Landing / resisting debuffs</td></tr><tr><td>Hit / Evasion</td><td>Whether an attack is evaded; separate from debuff accuracy</td></tr><tr><td>Toughness</td><td>The target’s break bar, not another HP pool</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The distinction between Hit and Effect Hit matters. An attack may connect while its debuff fails, and an evaded hit cannot apply that debuff at all. Diagnose the missing result before swapping gear.</p><h2>Worked example: compare mixed scaling</h2><p>Suppose a hypothetical single-target skill lists 115% ATK plus 3.5% Max HP. At 3,000 ATK and 20,000 HP, the two parts contribute 3,450 and 700, producing a 4,150 scaling subtotal. Adding 200 ATK raises that subtotal by 230. Adding 2,000 HP raises it by 70 while also making the user tougher. That does not make ATK universally better: survival or another HP-based skill may be the reason the unit is on the team.</p><p>This subtotal is not the final on-screen damage. The global damage factor, enemy DEF, Damage Up, critical effects, Battle Power and any special rule still follow. An all-enemy version divides ordinary ATK, HP and DEF scaling by 4 per target. When comparing two skills, match target type and assumptions first. Otherwise, a larger displayed percentage can appear to promise a result that the actual formula never gives.</p><h2>Choose the next useful stat</h2><p>Start with the failure you can observe. A carry dying before its turn needs protection or bulk. A support acting too late needs an action-order fix. A debuffer whose attack lands but whose effect fails may need Effect Hit or a better attribute matchup. A critical build with unreliable CRIT Rate does not become dependable simply by adding more CRIT DMG. This makes upgrade decisions smaller and easier to check.</p><p>Journey adds another layer. Strength turns into ATK, Vitality into HP, Endurance into DEF, Focus into Effect Hit and Protection into Effect RES, with role adjustments on core stats. Read the Journey guide before comparing raw training totals. Equipment percentages also apply through a staged stat calculation, so they should not all be added to the character’s bare base. Once the immediate problem is fixed, test the same encounter again before rebuilding the whole team.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Is Toughness another health bar?</summary><p>It is the bar that triggers Break when emptied. HP still decides whether the target survives.</p></details><details><summary>Does Effect Hit stop evasion?</summary><p>No. It belongs to the debuff check, not the attack’s evasion check.</p></details><details><summary>Should every Defender stack only HP?</summary><p>No. Read the protection and damage scaling, then account for DEF and required SPD.</p></details><details><summary>Is the scaling subtotal my final damage?</summary><p>No. Defense and the other damage rules still apply afterward.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/guide/systems-combat">Combat calculations</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/journey">Journey stat conversion</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-equipment">Equipment choices</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"systems-combat": {
		group: "systems",
		title: "Combat basics",
		sub: "Read damage, attribute advantage and debuff checks before changing your team.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Win fights by matching the skill to the target, then solving accuracy and timing. Attribute advantage adds 5% Damage Up and 30 percentage points of Effect Hit. Debuffs pass two checks. All-enemy ATK, HP and DEF scaling is divided by 4 per target. Critical damage joins the shared damage modifier pool. An evaded attack cannot remove Toughness.</p><h2>Choose the right kind of hit</h2><p>Sun beats Star, Star beats Moon, and Moon beats Sun; Order and Chaos each have advantage against the other. Advantage also contributes 1 base Toughness damage. Neutral and disadvantaged attacks start at 0, so a neutral attacker needs a separate Toughness bonus to break a target. Disadvantage subtracts 5% Damage Up and 30 percentage points of Effect Hit. These effects can make a reliable debuffer unreliable against the wrong attribute.</p><p>Check the target field before comparing multipliers. An all-enemy attack divides its ATK, HP and DEF factors by 4 even against a lone boss. A listed 265% ATK becomes 66.25% per target. SPD scaling, fixed damage and damage over time follow exceptions. A larger headline multiplier therefore does not automatically win a single-target race.</p><h2>Damage example: why the final hit is different</h2><p>Take a hypothetical single-target skill with 200% ATK scaling and an attacker with 2,500 ATK. Its initial 5,000 becomes 9,250 after the global 1.85 damage factor. A PvE target with 1,500 DEF reduces damage by 1,500 ÷ 4,500, leaving about 6,166.67. If the attacker then has a net 25% Damage Up modifier, the result is about 7,708.33 before rounding or any other effects. This example assumes no critical hit, Break, special boss rule or Battle Power penalty.</p><p>DEF mitigation uses DEF ÷ (DEF + 3,000) in PvE and DEF ÷ (DEF + 300) in PvP. Critical bonuses, skill-type bonuses and Damage Reduce share an additive modifier pool. The net negative modifier stops at −50%; the positive pool has no general upper cap. The CRIT DMG stat itself stops at 300%.</p><h2>Land a debuff before relying on it</h2><p>A debuff first rolls its printed activation chance, then checks Effect Hit against Effect RES, with the attribute adjustment included. Ordinary checks also face 20% Guaranteed Effect RES. More Effect Hit can overcome normal resistance, but cannot remove that guaranteed layer without a suitable Guaranteed Effect Hit effect. A printed 100% activation rate is therefore not the same as a guaranteed debuff.</p><p>For example, 120% Effect Hit against 60% Effect RES at attribute disadvantage gives a 30% hit check. If the skill activates only 75% of the time, its combined success chance is 0.75 × 0.30 = 22.5%. Check that risk before making DEF Down the only route to victory. Evasion adds 50 percentage points of Damage Reduce and prevents the evaded hit from applying debuffs or Toughness damage; it does not always halve the final damage.</p><h2>Healing, Chain damage and safe decisions</h2><p>Healing uses its stated scaling stat and heal rate, then adds outgoing and incoming healing bonuses in the same healing multiplier. A 50% ATK heal from 3,000 ATK with a combined 35% healing bonus restores 2,025 HP before other rules. A healer may therefore want ATK even though the party expects them to keep everyone alive. Read the actual skill instead of assigning every Supporter HP gear.</p><p>Chain damage is a separate hit that ignores DEF and does not critically strike. Damage Up and the Break bonus can still matter, while ordinary Damage Reduce and lifesteal do not work as they do on a normal attack. Against bosses, inspect their own damage caps and triggers as well. A rotation that succeeds on an ordinary enemy may feed a boss counter or waste its strongest hit into a protected turn.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Does advantage guarantee a debuff?</summary><p>No. It improves the Effect Hit check, but activation, resistance and evasion rules still matter.</p></details><details><summary>Does an AoE get stronger when enemies die?</summary><p>Its normal all-enemy ATK, HP and DEF divisor remains 4.</p></details><details><summary>Can every skill critically strike?</summary><p>Check the skill. Chain damage does not crit, and other special effects can have their own restrictions.</p></details><details><summary>Why is my damage lower than the example?</summary><p>Check Battle Power, enemy DEF, Damage Reduce, evasion, skill level and any boss-specific rule.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/guide/stats">Stats explained</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/team-building">Team building</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-break">Break and Nova Burst</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"systems-break": {
		group: "systems",
		title: "Break, Break Skills and Nova Burst",
		sub: "Turn a Toughness break into a planned damage window.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Plan the break before spending the burst. Attribute advantage supplies 1 base Toughness damage; neutral starts at 0. Break adds 15% damage vulnerability for 1 turn. A boss break earns 3 Break Skill points. A party equips 1 Break Skill. Nova Burst costs 4 Nova Force from a gauge that holds 12.</p><h2>What actually removes Toughness?</h2><p>Toughness is the bar you must empty to trigger Break. Start with the attack’s attribute contribution, add its extra Toughness damage, subtract the target’s reduction, and account for any fixed Toughness damage separately. An evaded or nullified attack cannot do the job. Neither can a non-attacking skill. A high ATK stat does not by itself make a skill a better breaker.</p><p>During Break, the target takes 15% increased damage and loses access to counters and Preemptive Guard for 1 turn. Monsters also have their Action gauge emptied; Saviors do not receive that gauge reset. This makes the same break useful for different reasons in PvE and PvP. Remove the last point shortly before your damage dealer acts, rather than spending the exposed turn on an unnecessary setup move.</p><h2>Choose the party Break Skill</h2><p>Breaking an ordinary enemy awards 1 point; breaking a boss awards 3. At 3 points, your selected Break Skill becomes ready for the next turn, then uses an 8-turn cooldown. Its party scaling rewards the whole roster, so replacing a teammate can change its output even when the user stays the same. Choose the active effect and the Domain together. The Domain is the continuing party bonus, not a second button you must remember to press.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Break Skill</th><th>Active effect</th><th>Domain</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Singularity Observation</td><td>70% combined team ATK to all enemies; +4 Nova Force</td><td>ATK and DEF +20%</td></tr><tr><td>Star Piercer</td><td>50% combined team ATK to all enemies; allies gain 20% Action gauge</td><td>CRIT Rate and CRIT DMG +10%; another +10% for Strikers/Rangers</td></tr><tr><td>Cube Overload</td><td>Heal using 2% combined team HP; ATK/Effect Hit +15% for 2 turns</td><td>SPD +10%; another +10% for Defenders/Supporters</td></tr><tr><td>Dimensional Assault</td><td>50% combined team ATK to all enemies; +4 Nova Force</td><td>ATK +15%; Toughness damage +1</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The all-enemy damage divisor still applies. Cube Overload supplies Omega Protocol; do not choose Star Piercer expecting that interaction.</p><h2>Worked example: line up Break and Nova Burst</h2><p>Imagine a boss has 2 Toughness remaining. Two ordinary advantageous hits with no extra reduction remove 1 each. The second hit breaks it and awards 3 points, making the party Break Skill available next turn. A neutral attacker without any extra Toughness effect would remove 0 instead. If the enemy reduces Toughness damage, recalculate before committing; do not assume those same two hits still work.</p><p>Now suppose the party has 0 Nova Force and uses Singularity Observation when available. Its +4 fills exactly one Nova Burst cost. Spend that burst on the next useful skill rather than waiting for the 12-point cap. The chosen skill receives 15% increased damage and the applicable fixed Toughness effect. Hyper skills can add a Savior-specific bonus. Check that bonus first: an ally-focused Nova Burst can differ from a normal attacking Hyper, so “every burst removes another point” is an unsafe shortcut.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Are Break and Break Skill the same thing?</summary><p>No. Break is the target state. A Break Skill is the party ability funded by breaking enemies.</p></details><details><summary>Does a broken Savior lose all Action gauge?</summary><p>No. That part of Break applies to monsters.</p></details><details><summary>Must Nova Force reach 12?</summary><p>No. You can spend at 4; 12 is the storage limit.</p></details><details><summary>Which Break Skill is always best?</summary><p>None. Compare the Domain, team scaling and whether you need gauge, Nova Force or healing.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/guide/systems-combat">Combat calculations</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/team-building">Team building</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-turns">Turn order</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"systems-turns": {
		group: "systems",
		title: "Turn order, positions and targeting",
		sub: "Make your support act before your carry and understand who can be hit.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Build a useful action order, then protect it. Action gauge fills to 10,000. SPD changes how quickly it fills. The PvE Leader gains 10 SPD. Standard teams have 4 slots across 2 rows. Enemy targeting uses skill-specific preferences. PvP opening speed varies by ±5%, or ±2.5% during pre-season.</p><h2>SPD is a clock, not an extra-turn stat</h2><p>Remaining time to act is (10,000 − current Action gauge) ÷ SPD. A faster Savior reaches the next turn sooner, but a gauge push can overtake raw speed. An extra turn is a skill effect, not simply the reward for reaching a certain SPD number. Recheck the order after a push, a delay, a death or a cooldown-changing effect; the opening list is not a permanent rotation.</p><p>The Leader receives 10 flat SPD in PvE. Account for that when arranging a buffer before a carry. In PvP, the opening uses a random speed factor between 0.95 and 1.05 for each unit, reduced to 0.975–1.025 during pre-season. Later turns do not repeat that opening jitter, although gauge manipulation still changes their order. A tiny speed lead is therefore not an opening guarantee.</p><h2>Worked example: a push beats higher SPD</h2><p>Savior A has 120 SPD and 4,000 gauge. Savior B has 100 SPD and 6,000 gauge. A needs 6,000 ÷ 120 = 50 time units, while B needs 4,000 ÷ 100 = 40. B acts first despite the lower SPD. If A receives a 20% gauge push, adding 2,000 gauge points, A instead needs about 33.33 time units and moves ahead. This assumes neither unit receives another effect before acting.</p><p>Use this when checking a planned buff window. It is not enough for the support to start faster: an ally’s automatic follow-up, an enemy pushback or a later gauge boost can separate the buff from the intended hit. Review the displayed order before spending a long cooldown. In PvP, leave room for the opening variance instead of treating two almost-equal speeds as a reliable setup.</p><h2>Rows reduce risk without granting safety</h2><p>A normal party contains 4 Saviors in front and back rows. A Journey party starts alone and can grow to 3, arranged as 1 front and 2 back. Put a durable unit where the enemy’s preferences tend to aim, and keep a fragile carry behind it. This is a useful default, not a promise that every enemy uses the same target rule.</p><p>Targeting preferences roll in sequence. A successful front-row preference narrows the candidate pool; a later successful Defender preference can narrow it again. A preference that would leave no valid target is ignored. That means an 80% front-row preference is not a universal 20% chance to hit every back-row Savior. Preemptive Guard can intercept eligible single-target attacks, but not all-enemy attacks, and Break disables the broken unit’s guard access temporarily. Read the particular enemy skill before changing formation.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Does the fastest unit always act next?</summary><p>No. Current Action gauge matters as well as SPD.</p></details><details><summary>Does back row block AoE?</summary><p>No. Row preference does not make an all-enemy skill single-target.</p></details><details><summary>Can I guarantee the PvP opener with 1 more SPD?</summary><p>No. Independent opening variance can reverse a small lead.</p></details><details><summary>Can I use the same speed order everywhere?</summary><p>Recheck it. PvE Leader SPD and mode-specific effects can change the sequence.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/guide/team-building">Team positions</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-pvp">PvP preparation</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-combat">Combat basics</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"systems-modes": {
		group: "systems",
		title: "Game modes",
		sub: "Choose the activity that pays for your next useful upgrade.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Pick a mode for its reward, then prepare for its rules. Cosmo Gate has 2 bosses with 3 steps each. Interstellar Corridor contains 850 floors. Cube Palace has 5 escalating stages and pays skill Cubes. Flash Point sets Saviors to level 200 and Resonance 10. Those rules do not make the modes equally easy.</p><h2>Choose a reward before choosing a fight</h2><p>Your next resource shortage is a better guide than the largest enemy level. Need skill Cubes? Work on Cube Palace progress. Need first-clear currency and Guidance Stones? Check the Corridor floor you can currently beat. Cosmo Gate asks you to divide a roster between bosses, while Flash Point removes part of the raw-level gap and tests the team you bring under its fixed rules.</p><p><a href="#/sectors">Campaign sectors</a> are a separate progression track. The local sector table stores stage counts and maximum battle levels, including 10 stages in Hard NOA Commercial District; those numbers are not universal recommendations for every mode. Use the enemy browser before committing a scarce entry. Check cleanse, counter and targeting triggers, then bring the matching tool. The difficulty badges below are editorial preparation labels, not an official universal rating.</p><h2>Cosmo Gate: prepare two teams</h2><article class="g-card"><div><h3>Cosmo Gate [Seasonal raid]</h3><span class="g-badge hard">Difficulty: Hard</span><p>Prepare separate boss lineups before locking in your strongest equipment. Each season has 2 bosses, each with 3 steps and a 50-turn limit. Saviors and gear committed to one boss cannot serve the other without a reset costing 1,000 Gold and erasing that boss score. Star Link growth beyond level 200 is excluded. Raid milestones pay contribution points and upgrade materials; ranking rewards include Stellagems and seasonal cosmetics. Bring sustained damage and answers to the selected boss: Rosaria takes extra damage from debuffs, while Hilde cleanses herself at turn start, making the same control plan unreliable against both.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Milestones reward total raid progress.</li><li>Challenge modifiers let prepared teams chase more score.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Roster and gear locks punish a one-team plan.</li><li>Extra modifier score is useless if the clear collapses.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating">Progression value: High once both teams can function</p></div></article><h2>Interstellar Corridor: collect repeatable milestones, not repeat rewards</h2><article class="g-card"><div><h3>Interstellar Corridor [First-clear tower]</h3><span class="g-badge medium">Difficulty: Medium</span><p>Treat each new floor as a small roster test. The general corridor has 250 floors, with another 200 each for Sun, Moon and Star, giving 850 in total. A floor’s first clear pays 30 Stellagems, 3,000 Gold and 5 Guidance Stones. The attribute corridors focus their matching stones. Bring enough protection for the current enemy and use attribute advantage when it helps Break and debuff reliability. The reward rule is per first clear, so farming a solved floor does not reproduce the entire payout. Stop at a genuine wall, improve the relevant build, then return.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Many separate first-clear rewards.</li><li>Attribute corridors supply focused Guidance Stones.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Later floors require stronger teams.</li><li>Solved floors do not repay first-clear currency.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating">Progression value: High for achievable new floors</p></div></article><h2>Cube Palace: turn progress into skill upgrades</h2><article class="g-card"><div><h3>Cube Palace [Skill Cube farming]</h3><span class="g-badge medium">Difficulty: Medium</span><p>Use Cube Palace when skill enhancement is the bottleneck. A Palace Access Card opens a run through 5 increasingly demanding stages. Progress makes Advanced, Expert, Master and Cosmic Cubes available. Computation Tier unlocks Cheat Codes that change battle effects, so read the current options before deciding what your lineup lacks. Bring reliable wave damage and enough sustain to keep moving under the turn limit. Incomplete runs can still produce rewards; the entry is settled when you finalize. Auto Protocol later farms up to your previously reached progress, making a better manual record useful beyond one attempt.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Partial progress still has value.</li><li>Auto Protocol reuses a stronger progress record.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>An entry card is required.</li><li>Wave clearing must also fit the turn limit.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating">Progression value: High when important skill breakpoints are waiting</p></div></article><h2>Flash Point: fixed levels still need a plan</h2><article class="g-card"><div><h3>Flash Point [Fixed-level boss challenge]</h3><span class="g-badge hard">Difficulty: Hard</span><p>Flash Point raises the battle roster to level 200 and Resonance 10. Entry uses a Grudgebound Challenge Letter. Manager Gregory, Carbonpunk Khan and Void Ascendant each have stages I–IV; I–III are sweepable, while IV adds Challenge of the Stars modifiers. Available battle buffs include 30% CRIT Rate, DEF or ATK alongside role bonuses. Bring a team that uses the selected buff and handles the chosen enemy’s skills. Treat its displayed reward list as the authority for the current stage: the mechanics summary verifies the entry and rules but does not provide a complete stage-by-stage reward inventory.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Fixed levels let more roster options participate.</li><li>Earlier stages support sweeping.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Fixed Resonance does not solve a poor matchup.</li><li>Stage IV modifiers can overturn an easy clear.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating">Progression value: Check the current reward preview before spending letters</p></div></article><h2>Worked example: add difficulty only when it pays</h2><p>At Cosmo Gate step 3, each of the 8 optional modifiers can add up to 30% score. All maximum choices add 240%, multiplying the base result by 3.4. A hypothetical 1,000,000-point result would become 3,400,000 only if the same underlying performance survived those settings. In practice, higher boss damage or reduced ally healing can lower survival and remaining-turn points, or cause failure outright.</p><p>Compare completed attempts rather than modifier totals. Start with a clear, add a setting your team can handle, and examine the final score. The same reasoning helps in Flash Point IV: choose the modifier that least disrupts your actual win condition. A fragile damage race should be cautious about enemy speed, while a slow recovery team must check healing reductions. The highest possible multiplier is an end target, not a sensible first attempt.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Which mode gives skill Cubes?</summary><p>Cube Palace is the dedicated mode described here.</p></details><details><summary>Do amplified levels help Cosmo Gate?</summary><p>Stat growth beyond Star Link level 200 does not apply to its boss battles.</p></details><details><summary>Are Corridor rewards paid again on every clear?</summary><p>The listed Stellagem, Gold and Guidance Stone bundle is a first-clear reward.</p></details><details><summary>Are Flash Point rewards fully listed here?</summary><p>No. Verify the current stage reward preview; the mechanics summary does not enumerate every reward.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/enemies">Enemy skills</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-power">Battle Power</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/team-building">Team building</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"systems-pvp": {
		group: "systems",
		title: "PvP guide",
		sub: "Prepare for the two arenas, Rotation Ban and the opening speed roll.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Build a flexible arena roster rather than copying a single usage list. Async fights AI defense teams; Ranked is real time. Ranked draft begins at Gold. Rotation Ban starts at Platinum and reduces selected stats by 10% per stack. Ranked fixes level 200 and Resonance 10. The usage shown here is a stored snapshot, not live telemetry.</p><h2>Choose your arena and understand scaling</h2><p>Async Arena uses your attack team against an AI-controlled defense. It holds up to 6 tickets, recovering 1 every 4 hours, and awards weekly tier rewards. Ranked Arena is a real-time match with a 20-second decision timer and the listed 5-second delay allowance. Draft starts at Gold, so prepare alternatives before relying on one irreplaceable carry. Ranked normalizes level and Resonance to 200 and 10.</p><p>Both modes compress HP, ATK and DEF by stat source; secondary stats are not included in that compression. In Ranked, base, Stellar Archive and equipment contributions each use 10%, while Tactics and Star Link contributions use 0%. Strategy Battle uses 10% base, Archive and gear, plus 5% Tactics and Star Link. The DEF formula uses a 300 constant. Do not transfer a PvE damage estimate directly into the arena.</p><h2>Rotation Ban: a penalty you can recover from</h2><p>Rotation Ban changes a Savior’s stats instead of removing them from selection. It applies at Platinum and above. Ranked chooses 3 Saviors by the previous week’s win rate; Strategy Battle chooses 3 by participation count. Tallying begins in the first season week, with the first penalties applied in the second. A name popular in this guide’s snapshot is not proof that it is banned in your current week.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Consecutive selected weeks</th><th>Penalty to HP, ATK, DEF, SPD, Effect Hit and Effect RES</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>−10%</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>−20%</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>−30%</td></tr><tr><td>4 or more</td><td>−40% cap</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Once a Savior leaves the selection, the penalty recovers one stage each week. Season changes reset the target list. Worked example: a hypothetical 120 SPD becomes 96 under a 20% penalty, before other changes. One recovery step takes that penalty to 10%, giving 108 SPD. Check the current ban stage before using a saved opening order.</p><h2>Tier ladder and the cost of waiting</h2><p>The shared ladder names are Bronze, Silver, Gold, Platinum, Diamond, Master, Grandmaster and Eternium. The point thresholds differ between modes. Bronze begins at division 6; Silver through Grandmaster use 5 divisions. The table shows the entry to division 5, except Bronze and Eternium. These are rank entry thresholds, not hard caps on your score. Exact win and loss changes also include a server-set base amount.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Rank entry</th><th>Async score</th><th>Ranked score</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bronze 6</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr><tr><td>Silver 5</td><td>400</td><td>250</td></tr><tr><td>Gold 5</td><td>1,000</td><td>500</td></tr><tr><td>Platinum 5</td><td>2,000</td><td>1,000</td></tr><tr><td>Diamond 5</td><td>3,000</td><td>1,500</td></tr><tr><td>Master 5</td><td>4,000</td><td>2,500</td></tr><tr><td>Grandmaster 5</td><td>5,000</td><td>3,500</td></tr><tr><td>Eternium</td><td>6,000</td><td>4,500</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ranked inactivity decay starts at Diamond. Async opponent refreshes escalate from 1,000 Gold for the first refresh to 100,000 for the tenth. Spend refreshes to solve a specific matchup rather than casually rerolling the list; a ticket and a plausible plan are worth more than a familiar-looking enemy name.</p><h2>Sudden death changes the win condition</h2><p>Async sudden death damages the attacking team, beginning at turn 25 with 300 fixed Chain damage. Later strikes arrive more often and grow stronger. A defense built only to survive can exploit that clock, so your attacking team still needs a route to finish. Ranked instead uses Roche Limit on both sides. Turn 13 marks its start without a stat change; the first active stage arrives at turn 22.</p><p>At turn 22, Ranked gains 10% Damage Up and Effect Hit while HP, DEF and healing fall by 10%. By turn 43, the listed stage reaches 80% Damage Up, 75% Effect Hit, −60% HP and healing, and −40% DEF. Each stage replaces the previous one. These shifts make endless healing a poor sole win condition. Plan which enemy to remove while your recovery tools still work, and preserve damage access rather than spending every turn repairing a losing position.</p><h2>Usage snapshot and three team ideas</h2><p>The local snapshot stores participation values but does not record the sample size, collection time or arena split. The check date below means the file was reviewed on 13/11/2026. It does not turn the numbers into today’s win rates. Use these values to find relevant kits to read, then check current bans and your opponent. The three plans below are kit-based suggestions, not measured matchups or tested best-in-slot teams.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Savior</th><th>Stored usage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="#/savior/summer-oracle">Luna (White Pearl Trap)</a></td><td>87%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/wedding-shielder">Carmen (Eternal Promise)</a></td><td>85%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/independent-vagabond">Fei (Wanderer)</a></td><td>53%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/maid-lantern">Carnelia (Wanderer)</a></td><td>48%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/stranisearl-roseblade">Amora (House Stranis)</a></td><td>43%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/west-beamsword">Ceres (Western Liberators)</a></td><td>17%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/counterside-hilde">Hilde (Fenrir Squad)</a></td><td>16%</td></tr><tr><td><a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily (Candle Square)</a></td><td>13%</td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Control and finish:</b> <a href="#/savior/summer-oracle">Luna (White Pearl Trap)</a> (87%), <a href="#/savior/maid-lantern">Carnelia (Wanderer)</a> (48%), <a href="#/savior/wedding-shielder">Carmen (Eternal Promise)</a> (85%) and <a href="#/savior/west-beamsword">Ceres (Western Liberators)</a> (17%). Set up pressure, protect the finisher and check debuff resistance.</p><p><b>Survive and punish:</b> <a href="#/savior/wedding-shielder">Carmen (Eternal Promise)</a>, <a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily (Candle Square)</a> (13%), <a href="#/savior/independent-vagabond">Fei (Wanderer)</a> (53%) and <a href="#/savior/stranisearl-roseblade">Amora (House Stranis)</a> (43%). Trade protection for a slower finish; watch sudden death.</p><p><b>Debuff pressure:</b> <a href="#/savior/summer-oracle">Luna (White Pearl Trap)</a>, <a href="#/savior/maid-lantern">Carnelia (Wanderer)</a>, <a href="#/savior/detective-professor">Professor M (Lampadis)</a> (11%) and <a href="#/savior/wedding-shielder">Carmen (Eternal Promise)</a>. Stack useful disruption while retaining a durable slot; cleanse and resistance can break this plan.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Is 87% usage an 87% win rate?</summary><p>No. Usage measures participation in the stored source, with no verified sample window here.</p></details><details><summary>Does Rotation Ban affect every stat?</summary><p>No. Its six listed stats are HP, ATK, DEF, SPD, Effect Hit and Effect RES.</p></details><details><summary>Do I get draft in Bronze Ranked?</summary><p>The documented draft threshold is Gold.</p></details><details><summary>Does Roche Limit damage only the attacker?</summary><p>No. Ranked applies its shifts to both teams; Async has a different attacker-side sudden death rule.</p></details><details><summary>Can a small SPD lead guarantee the first turn?</summary><p>No. The opening roll can overturn it. Read the turn-order guide.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/guide/meta">Current rating snapshot</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-turns">Turn order</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/team-building">Build a flexible team</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"systems-equipment": {
		group: "systems",
		title: "Equipment and gear score",
		sub: "Choose the right main stat, useful set and meaningful substats before chasing a score.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Build gear for the skill’s scaling and the team’s turn order. There are 6 equipment slots and 12 sets. Boots always have SPD as their main stat. Speed Set gives 15 flat SPD at 4 pieces. Gear score weights rolled substats; it does not measure team fit. A high score cannot rescue an unwanted main stat.</p><h2>Start with the main stat and a working set</h2><p>Weapon, Gauntlets, Armor and Boots have fixed main-stat types. Amulets and Rings offer choices, making their main stat a major build decision. Check whether your damage or healing scales with ATK, HP or DEF before choosing. SPD on an Amulet competes with damage and durability, while a Ring can supply Effect Hit or Effect RES. A support who must buff before the carry may value turn order more than a larger heal on a late turn.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Slot</th><th>Possible main stats</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Weapon</td><td>Flat ATK</td></tr><tr><td>Gauntlets</td><td>Flat HP</td></tr><tr><td>Armor</td><td>Flat DEF</td></tr><tr><td>Boots</td><td>Flat SPD</td></tr><tr><td>Amulet</td><td>ATK%, HP%, DEF%, SPD, CRIT Rate, CRIT DMG</td></tr><tr><td>Ring</td><td>ATK%, HP%, DEF%, Effect Hit, Effect RES</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Four-piece sets leave room for one two-piece set. A pair of unrelated pieces does not activate a two-piece bonus. Check the completed combination before comparing the individual items; a small substat gain can be outweighed by losing a set that makes the rotation function.</p><h2>Which sets fit which job?</h2><p>Start with the skill’s scaling, accuracy needs and turn order. A critical carry short of CRIT Rate may prefer Insight; a debuffer may need Hit first. Health supports HP scaling. Speed Set grants 15 flat SPD, not 15%. These are starting points, not universal best-in-slot claims.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Set</th><th>Pieces</th><th>Bonus</th><th>Who wants it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Speed Set</td><td>4</td><td>SPD +15</td><td>Openers and supports that must act first</td></tr><tr><td>Defense Set</td><td>2</td><td>DEF% +15%</td><td>DEF-scaling builds needing durability</td></tr><tr><td>Hit Set</td><td>2</td><td>Effect Hit +20%</td><td>Debuffers still missing the required Effect Hit</td></tr><tr><td>Resistance Set</td><td>2</td><td>Effect RES +20%</td><td>Supports whose job fails when controlled</td></tr><tr><td>Attack Set</td><td>4</td><td>ATK% +20%</td><td>ATK-scaling damage or healing builds</td></tr><tr><td>Insight Set</td><td>4</td><td>CRIT Rate +30%</td><td>Critical carries short of reliable CRIT Rate</td></tr><tr><td>Valor Set</td><td>2</td><td>ATK% +5%, Effect RES +5%</td><td>ATK builds wanting a small resistance side benefit</td></tr><tr><td>Precision Set</td><td>4</td><td>CRIT Rate +15%, Effect Hit +20%</td><td>Critical attackers who also need to land debuffs</td></tr><tr><td>Health Set</td><td>4</td><td>HP% +30%</td><td>HP-scaling tanks or healers</td></tr><tr><td>Destruction Set</td><td>4</td><td>CRIT DMG +40%</td><td>Critical carries that already have enough CRIT Rate</td></tr><tr><td>Barrier Set</td><td>2</td><td>HP% +10%, Effect RES +5%</td><td>HP builds needing a resistance side benefit</td></tr><tr><td>Annihilation Set</td><td>4</td><td>CRIT DMG +20%, Effect Hit +20%</td><td>Critical debuff attackers with enough CRIT Rate</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The equipment database groups several sets under boss families. Those families are sometimes called “Motivator” or “Wendigo” sets. Those labels do not identify a single bonus. Use the exact set name and piece requirement above when following a build.</p><h2>Prioritize the stat the role actually uses</h2><p>For an ATK-based Striker, Ranger, Caster or Assassin, begin with ATK and enough CRIT Rate for the chosen rotation, then add CRIT DMG. Keep the SPD needed to take useful turns. For a debuff-focused version of those roles, Effect Hit can outrank another damage roll until the target check is reliable. Do not use class alone to choose: mixed HP or DEF scaling can change that order.</p><p>For a Defender, start with the stat named in the protection or damage skill, then the other bulk stat and useful SPD. Add resistance if keeping guard or recovery available is the job. For a Supporter, use the actual heal or Barrier scaling, preserve action order, and add resistance or Effect Hit only when the kit needs it. <a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily (Candle Square)</a> is an HP-scaling example, while an ATK-scaling healer should not inherit her exact build. These are kit-based priorities, not a claim that every role needs the same numbered target.</p><h2>Worked example: what gear score measures</h2><p>The local scorer multiplies each substat by a weight, then adds the results. A hypothetical piece with 1.6% ATK, 2 SPD, 2% CRIT Rate and 3% CRIT DMG scores 12 + 12 + 13.2 + 13.2 = 50.4. The weights are 7.5 per percentage point of ATK, 6 per SPD, 6.6 per CRIT Rate point and 4.4 per CRIT DMG point. Enter 1.6 for 1.6%, not 0.016.</p><p>That total is a roll-quality measure within this site’s model, not an official damage rating. The same 50.4 points may be excellent for a carry and poorly aimed for a defensive healer. Enhancement level also changes the comparison: the local ceilings run from 52.8 at +0 to 118.8 at +15. T2 substat ranges are specific to T2 equipment. Compare similar item levels, check the main stat and set, then ask whether the rolled stats serve the wearer.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Can Boots roll SPD as a substat?</summary><p>The slot table excludes it: Boots already have SPD as the main stat.</p></details><details><summary>Is Speed Set a percentage bonus?</summary><p>No. Its four-piece bonus is 15 flat SPD.</p></details><details><summary>Should I use the highest gear score automatically?</summary><p>No. The main stat, set completion, scaling and required action order come first.</p></details><details><summary>Are T2 roll ranges valid for every item tier?</summary><p>No. The database explicitly labels those ranges T2.</p></details><details><summary>Can every Ring have CRIT Rate main stat?</summary><p>No. Rings offer ATK%, HP%, DEF%, Effect Hit or Effect RES.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/gearscore">Gear score calculator</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/stats">Stats explained</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-turns">Turn order</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"systems-power": {
		group: "systems",
		title: "Stats, Battle Power and star grade",
		sub: "Fix the PvE power penalty without mistaking a score for a complete build.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Use Battle Power to clear the stage penalty, then judge the kit and rotation. PvE has no penalty at 90% of recommended Battle Power. At 80%, you deal 80% damage and take 120%. Stats pass through 2 percentage stages. Each star adds 2% HP, ATK and DEF. Battle Power keeps the highest of 3 profiles.</p><h2>How a final stat is assembled</h2><p>The calculation first gathers base, growth and Journey stats. It adds per-level growth and applies the first percentage stage. Flat additions such as equipment then enter the pool before the second percentage stage. The final whole-number stat rounds down. A late percentage bonus can therefore amplify flat equipment; it is not always a percentage of the bare character base. Rate stats, such as CRIT Rate, use direct percentage-point additions instead.</p><p>As a simple example, take 1,000 base ATK plus 200 growth, no first-stage bonus, and 500 flat equipment ATK. A later 15% ATK bonus multiplies 1,700, giving 1,955 ATK. Applying it only to 1,200 would undercount the equipment interaction. Each star contributes another 2% to HP, ATK and DEF through its appropriate stat bonus, reaching 14% at 7 stars; stars do not give every secondary stat that bonus.</p><h2>Worked example: escaping a stage penalty</h2><p>A stage recommends 100,000 Battle Power. At 80,000, the listed PvE rule makes you deal 80% normal damage while enemies deal 120%. At 90,000, both return to 100%. Against otherwise identical conditions, crossing from 80% output to full output is a 25% increase in your effective damage. That is why a useful upgrade can feel much larger than its printed ATK gain when it also crosses a power threshold.</p><p>There is no extra damage reward for going above the recommendation. Use the number as a gate check, not as proof that a mismatched team should win. A team at the safe threshold can still lack healing, lose its debuff check or trigger a boss counter. Below 50% of recommended power, the listed floor is 10% damage dealt and 190% taken. PvP does not use this Battle Power penalty system.</p><h2>Why the highest score can mislead</h2><p>The game evaluates attack-focused, balanced and critical-focused Battle Power profiles and uses the largest result. HP, DEF, ATK, SPD, critical stats, Effect Hit and Effect RES all feed the calculation. A stat that lifts the displayed score may still do little for the job you need: resistance does not replace a missing heal, and unused Effect Hit does not strengthen an attack-only carry’s damage.</p><p>The final multiplier includes a fixed 6% term and 1% per summed skill level across the four skills. The fixed term does not change with the Savior’s own Limit Break level. Only Potential points marked for Battle Power enter the separate Potential factor. Read the actual effect of an upgrade before spending just to raise the score. Once the stage penalty is gone, improve scaling, accuracy and action order in ways the team will actually use.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Should I exceed the stage recommendation?</summary><p>It can still make the team stronger, but the Battle Power multiplier gives no over-cap damage bonus.</p></details><details><summary>Does every Potential point raise Battle Power?</summary><p>No. Only points from nodes flagged for Battle Power enter that factor.</p></details><details><summary>Do stars increase SPD by 2%?</summary><p>The star-grade bonus described here applies to HP, ATK and DEF.</p></details><details><summary>Is the fixed 6% factor my own Limit Break bonus?</summary><p>No. The documented formula uses the maximum constant for every Savior.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/guide/stats">Stats explained</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/journey">Journey upgrades</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/systems-equipment">Equipment decisions</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"story-guide": {
		group: "start",
		title: "Story guide",
		sub: "Follow the main plot, choose character side stories and control spoilers.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Read the Prologue and main episodes in order, then choose side stories around the people you know. The local library contains 8 main entries, 16 events, 59 Savior stories and 1 Journey story. Summaries describe plot events and can spoil them. The overall summary reaches later main episodes, so avoid it when starting fresh.</p><h2>Four story types, four reasons to read</h2><p>Main stories carry the Captain and Voyager party through the central plot. Their episode labels give the clearest reading sequence. Event stories follow named side arcs, often with a different focus or cast. Savior stories explain an individual version’s relationships, choices and everyday circumstances. The Journey story follows the training adventure as a narrative rather than serving as a menu of training rules.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Local entries</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Main</td><td>8, including the Prologue</td><td>The central plot in episode order</td></tr><tr><td>Event</td><td>16</td><td>A named side arc and its featured cast</td></tr><tr><td>Savior</td><td>59</td><td>A particular Savior version and personal perspective</td></tr><tr><td>Journey</td><td>1</td><td>Pathfinder of Starlight’s story sequence</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These counts describe the stories stored here. They do not claim that every story ever released is present. A version title matters: an event costume or later identity can have its own story even when the name matches an earlier Savior.</p><h2>A reading order that keeps introductions clear</h2><p>Start with the Prologue, then follow episodes 1 through 7 using their labels. This is the library’s main narrative order, not a claim about the release dates of every event. Read chapters in their displayed order inside each entry. The guide does not need a speculative chronology to explain what comes next: the episode sequence already gives a dependable first pass through the central story.</p><ol class="g-steps"><li><a href="#/story/main-episode-0">Prologue: At the End of Starlight</a>.</li><li><a href="#/story/main-episode-1">Episode 1: Lavistar</a>.</li><li><a href="#/story/main-episode-2">Episode 2: Happy Smile</a>.</li><li><a href="#/story/main-episode-3">Episode 3: Monolith Order</a>.</li><li><a href="#/story/main-episode-4">Episode 4: The Name on the Gravestone</a>.</li><li><a href="#/story/main-episode-5">Episode 5: Lingering Wings on the Street</a>.</li><li><a href="#/story/main-episode-6">Episode 6: Rhapsody of Unbroken Blades</a>.</li><li><a href="#/story/main-episode-7">Episode 7: The Witch&#x27;s Veil</a>.</li></ol><p>After meeting a character in the main plot, take their Savior story as a side read. Choose events when you recognize the featured cast, or deliberately accept that they may introduce later relationships. Return to the next main episode afterward. This approach preserves introductions without claiming that every side arc happens between two exact campaign chapters.</p><h2>Which Savior stories are worth reading first?</h2><p>Start with the Voyager versions of Asherah, Smile and Luna if you want more context for the main party. Their personal stories add perspective to people the early campaign already asks you to follow. If a major supporting character interests you, read that version next rather than treating the roster as mandatory homework. You do not need to finish all 59 Savior entries before continuing the main plot.</p><p>Pay special attention to the full title when a name repeats. Charlotte (Heart of Monastir) and Charlotte (Bunnygirl) are different entries; Luna (Voyager Savior Party) and Luna (White Pearl Trap) also lead to different perspectives. The same care helps when moving between the Western Liberators stories and their event versions. These are reading recommendations based on the local summaries, not promises that a particular side story unlocks a combat reward or is required for progression.</p><h2>Spoiler policy: summaries are still spoilers</h2><p>The library has a high-level overall recap, a summary for each story and shorter chapter summaries. All three can reveal events. The overall recap reaches the later main plot; it is useful for returning readers but is the riskiest starting point for someone new. A chapter summary can also reveal the resolution of that scene even when it uses only a few sentences.</p><p>For a first reading, use episode labels and titles to navigate, then read the corresponding source story before opening its recap. For a refresher, stop at the last chapter you actually finished. Summary text here is original prose rather than copied dialogue, but that does not make its plot information spoiler-free. The guide keeps its own recommendations broad and avoids explaining late twists. Story order claims use the local episode structure; unsupported event release dates and a complete cross-event timeline remain unconfirmed.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Should I read the overall summary first?</summary><p>Only if later-main-story spoilers are acceptable. New readers should start with the Prologue.</p></details><details><summary>Are all Savior stories required?</summary><p>No narrative requirement is established here. Read the main party and the characters that interest you.</p></details><details><summary>Does the list prove event chronology?</summary><p>No. It is a library order, not a verified release or in-world timeline.</p></details><details><summary>Why does the same name have multiple stories?</summary><p>Different Savior titles represent different versions. Choose the full name and title.</p></details><details><summary>Is the Journey story the training guide?</summary><p>No. Use the Journey guide for mechanics and Pathfinder of Starlight for its narrative.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/stories">Open the story library</a>.</li><li><a href="#/story/main-episode-0">Start the Prologue</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/journey">Journey mechanics</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"pull-guide": {
		group: "start",
		title: "First pulls and who to chase",
		sub: "Eight useful starting jobs, with the conditions each candidate needs.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Pull to complete a party, then improve its strongest working core. Prioritize protection, recovery and one reliable attacker across four slots. The eight candidates below cover different jobs; their published PvE and PvP ratings are separate, and their availability on today\'s banners is unconfirmed. Do not budget around an assumed free selector, advertised reroll time or an old launch reward.</p><h2>Is rerolling worth it?</h2><p>The current reset procedure, tutorial duration, opening-ticket total and banner guarantees are unconfirmed in this guide. Older community articles recommend different targets because they describe different release periods. That is not proof that one current banner offers every option. If you keep your account, choose an achievable four-slot party; if you restart, inspect the in-game account rules and active observation details before discarding progress.</p><div class="callout warn">This is a kit-based shortlist, not a live banner ranking. No pull rate, pity count or account-deletion procedure is asserted.</div><h2>Which eight saviors offer useful starting jobs?</h2><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/icons/UFS_NKM_UNIT_S_MAID_TWOHANDER.webp" alt=""><div><h3><a href="#/savior/maid-twohander">Emily · Candle Square</a> [Tank]</h3><p>Emily is an HP-scaling defender who protects a chosen attacker while building repeated area damage. Cleaning Service supplies ATK Up and a Barrier without giving up Emily&#x27;s next action, and incoming attacks reduce her ultimate cooldown. Turn-Down Service builds Awakened Memory, making later ultimates more threatening.</p><p>Her targeted buff advances her own action rather than leaving a long setup gap. HP improves several parts of her job, which makes defensive investment productive. She is a protector first: a party that cannot finish enemies still needs an attacker. Incoming hits help her ultimate cycle, but her listed kit does not guarantee that enemies will select her.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Targeted ATK buff and Barrier come with Emily&#x27;s own extra turn.</li><li>Incoming attacks reduce a powerful area ultimate&#x27;s cooldown.</li><li>HP investment helps protection, damage and survival together.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>No actual cleanse or ordinary heal is listed.</li><li>No Taunt guarantees attacks into her cooldown engine.</li><li>Ultimate starts on a long cooldown and grows stronger over repeated uses.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Early game value:</b> High when one carry needs protection.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/icons/UFS_NKM_UNIT_S_DRESS_STRANIS.webp" alt=""><div><h3><a href="#/savior/dress-stranis">Asherah · Waltz of Starlight</a> [Healer]</h3><p>Dress Asherah is an HP-scaling healer and cleanser, not the damage-focused Voyager or Dark variants. Bal Doré heals while attacking; Lumière Sonnet heals the party, removes two debuffs and helps other allies reach their next turns. Her upgraded passive makes basic attacks contribute targeted recovery.</p><p>Use her when repeated incoming damage and removable debuffs stop your clears. Her healing scales with her own HP, giving a straightforward survival priority. She offers a different job from offensive Asherah variants, so check the title rather than choosing by the shared name. She cannot undo a lethal hit that arrives before her recovery action.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Party healing and two-debuff cleanse share one skill.</li><li>Can heal while attacking with Bal Doré and, at upgraded passive levels, her basic.</li><li>HP investment improves recovery and durability together; Sonnet also advances other allies.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>No revive, barrier or complete prevention of incoming debuffs.</li><li>Basic recovery and high dual-attack chance require later passive mechanics.</li><li>Healing cannot save a teammate defeated before she acts, and two removals may not clear a full debuff stack.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Early game value:</b> High for sustained progression.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/icons/UFS_NKM_UNIT_S_BUNNY_KNIGHT.webp" alt=""><div><h3><a href="#/savior/bunny-knight">Charlotte · Heart of Monastir</a> [Main DPS]</h3><p>Bunny Charlotte is a Sun single-target attacker who also buffs the party&#x27;s ATK. Song of the Toast grants a DEF-penetrating Lucky Token and an extra turn; while the token lasts, her own-turn basic repeats once. Sun teammates help refill Ignition for Cornered Rabbit.</p><p>She is a focused damage option rather than a complete party by herself. Her token creates a useful setup-and-attack sequence, while Sun turns help her resource economy. If your available supports are mostly another attribute, read the dependency note before assuming the published tier describes your result. Her lack of broad wave damage is a real cost in multi-target stages.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Team ATK buff and an extra setup turn combine offense with support.</li><li>Lucky Token grants undispellable DEF penetration.</li><li>Sun supports generate her resource without abandoning their normal jobs.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>No area attack, cleanse or ordinary healing.</li><li>Sun turns and Lucky Token uptime heavily influence her output.</li><li>The reset text is internally awkward; lower-level Song has random stack generation.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Early game value:</b> High with Sun support.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/icons/UFS_NKM_UNIT_S_INDEPENDENT_DRAGON.webp" alt=""><div><h3><a href="#/savior/independent-dragon">Lacy · Wanderer</a> [Main DPS]</h3><p>Lacy combines fast single-target attacks with a team-wide Entangled Dream debuff. That mark weakens enemy ATK and makes attacks into Lacy feed her healing and next-turn progress. She has substantial personal tools, but the response loop only works when marked enemies actually attack her.</p><p>Her personal sustain can reduce the pressure on recovery, but it does not replace healing for the other three slots. The enemy mark connects offense, incoming attacks and her response engine. A tank that redirects everything away from Lacy can weaken that interaction. Against mark immunity, judge the remaining kit rather than counting on the full loop.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>High listed maximum SPD and five Toughness support an active personal kit.</li><li>Ultimate applies a broad ATK-reducing mark while damaging a chosen enemy.</li><li>Marked attacks into Lacy feed healing, resource and next-turn progress together.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>No party heal or direct ally protection.</li><li>Mark immunity removes multiple connected benefits.</li><li>An ally&#x27;s Taunt can divert attacks away from her self-recovery engine.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Early game value:</b> High if enemies can trigger her mark.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/icons/UFS_NKM_UNIT_S_BUNNY_SPEAR.webp" alt=""><div><h3><a href="#/savior/bunny-spear">Claire · Flawless Blue Rose</a> [Main DPS]</h3><p>Bunny Claire is a focused Moon attacker whose crits generate resources and faster turns. Bunny Jackpot starts a three-turn crit buff, Where Are You Looking? adds three Chill on a crit, and a basic used at full Chill can trigger that special automatically.</p><p>Choose her for concentrated pressure into a priority target. Her critical hits and Chill feed one another, so a build missing reliable critical chance can feel much slower than a finished showcase. She does not solve wide enemy waves alone. A partner who duplicates an already-sufficient critical buff may be less valuable than protection or a different damage multiplier.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Can maintain focused pressure with crit-driven Chill and a triggered special.</li><li>Own critical buffs reduce long-fight dependence on a permanent external crit supporter.</li><li>Bunny Jackpot specifically rewards attacking buffed enemies.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>No area attack for broad wave clear.</li><li>Failure to crit the special slows resource generation.</li><li>Full passive crit, Jackpot Bloom and external crit buffs can overlap wastefully.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Early game value:</b> High with reliable critical hits.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/icons/UFS_NKM_UNIT_S_MAID_BELL.webp" alt=""><div><h3><a href="#/savior/maid-bell">Bell · Candle Square</a> [Debuffer]</h3><p>Bell is a fast control attacker whose main value is disrupting enemy buffs and cooldown skills. Condolence hits the whole team, lowers ACC when it lands and grants Bell SPD Up. Encoffin strips one target&#x27;s buffs and attempts Silence, while any other unit&#x27;s defeat feeds her next-turn progress and ATK.</p><p>Bell earns a place when disrupting an enemy turn matters more than adding another uncomplicated attacker. Her broad accuracy pressure and targeted control address different threats. Boss immunity or undispellable effects can remove much of that utility, so inspect the enemy before investing for a specific wall. Her defeat-triggered tempo also behaves differently in a long single-boss battle.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Combines broad ACC Down with targeted buff removal and Silence.</li><li>Defeats advance her turn even when someone else scores the kill.</li><li>Her special provides both resource buildup and personal SPD Up.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Area ACC Down remains probabilistic even at the highest listed level.</li><li>Silence and dispel are much less useful against immune or undispellable effects.</li><li>No self-heal or Barrier protects an offensive build.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Early game value:</b> Useful against removable buffs and silence targets.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/icons/UFS_NKM_UNIT_S_WEDDING_SHIELDER.webp" alt=""><div><h3><a href="#/savior/wedding-shielder">Carmen · Eternal Promise</a> [Support]</h3><p>Wedding Carmen protects the highest-ATK ally selected at the first battle’s start. Damage sharing, automatic rescue and party recovery on her basic create a sustained carry-protection kit, while Wedding March provides a targeted cleanse/heal and feeds Chill to Moon allies.</p><p>She asks you to inspect party ATK before entering battle: the oath is assigned at the start rather than freely moved after a mistake. Her protection is substantial, but damage sharing transfers a survival obligation onto Carmen. Keeping her alive matters just as much as protecting the carry. Treat her targeted recovery separately from a full-party cleanse.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Combines damage sharing with automatic heal/Barrier rescue for the oath carry.</li><li>Party recovery on basics gives useful actions between cooldowns.</li><li>Targeted full cleanse and Moon Chill feed support several different carries.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>ATK ordering can assign the oath to the wrong ally.</li><li>Redirected damage creates a real survival burden for Carmen.</li><li>Reaction cooldown prevents every incoming hit from receiving a fresh rescue.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Early game value:</b> High for protecting one correctly selected carry.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/icons/UFS_NKM_UNIT_S_EASTERN_SWORDMASTER.webp" alt=""><div><h3><a href="#/savior/eastern-swordmaster">Lyn · Wind Moon Sect</a> [Main DPS]</h3><p>Lyn is a Star attacker with two purposeful branches. Phantom Blade Dance first grants Bright Moon for a stronger focused ultimate; Clear Wind Bright Moon first stays area damage and grants Star-team crit-damage support plus Clear Wind, enabling later Leap support. Skill order changes what the team gets.</p><p>Lyn is more demanding to pilot than a single linear damage sequence. One branch favors focused damage; the other preserves area coverage and Star-team support. That flexibility is valuable only when you choose the branch the encounter needs. If you are learning the game, read both skill-order outcomes before committing rare materials or expecting an automatic rotation to make the same choice.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Can choose area support or stronger focused damage through actual skill order.</li><li>Clear Wind branch distributes Leap and crit damage support to Star allies.</li><li>Conditional extra turns can connect the two skills when both are ready.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Wrong branch can trade away needed wave coverage or team buffs.</li><li>Extra turns depend on paired-skill availability.</li><li>No healing, cleanse or protection, and focused critical payoff needs reliable crits.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Early game value:</b> High for a planned Star party.</p></div></div><h2>FAQ</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Is this the same as the tier list?</summary><p>No. Early value includes role coverage and ease of building, not only endgame mode ratings.</p></details><details><summary>Which Asherah is the healer?</summary><p>Waltz of Starlight, slug dress-stranis. Other versions have different kits.</p></details><details><summary>Do I need duplicates immediately?</summary><p>Read the individual star-grade and skill changes first. This page does not assert a universal copy requirement.</p></details><details><summary>What about Arcana?</summary><p>Their training effects and potentials support repeated Journeys; compare them with the missing combat role before spending flexible currency.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/tiers">Mode tiers</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/arcana-guide">Arcana guide</a>.</li><li><a href="#/saviors">Full skills and profiles</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"arcana-guide": {
		group: "start",
		title: "Arcana Guide",
		sub: "Choose a training package by stat, bond condition and the leader’s kit.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		html: '<p class="lead">Pick Arcana for the training your leader needs, then check the effects at your card’s level. Five tags cover Strength, Vitality, Endurance, Focus and Protection. A matching party character adds 5% Training Effect; a matching training tag only enables the Sensory chance roll. Super Sensory also requires the card’s bond threshold, commonly 30–70. Stored card tiers are suggestions, not guaranteed outcomes.</p><h2>What do Arcana actually do?</h2><p>Arcana shape Journey through starting bonuses, training modifiers, event rewards and potentials. These effects operate at different times: a Journey Start effect is not a bonus applied after every training session. Read the effect condition and unlock level together in the <a href="#/arcana">Arcana database</a>. The local card data can show lower-level values than the current live DB table, which displays different leveled totals. Compare the same card level before calling that a contradiction. Your leader’s damage, recovery and protection scaling should decide the stat package before a tier letter does.</p><h2>Which stat should I train?</h2><table><tr><th>Tag</th><th>Combat goal</th><th>Typical user</th></tr><tr><td>Strength</td><td>ATK</td><td>ATK-scaling attacker</td></tr><tr><td>Vitality</td><td>HP</td><td>HP-scaling healer or protector</td></tr><tr><td>Endurance</td><td>DEF</td><td>Survival or DEF scaling</td></tr><tr><td>Focus</td><td>Effect Hit</td><td>Debuff-dependent kit</td></tr><tr><td>Protection</td><td>Effect RES</td><td>Resistance against relevant enemy effects</td></tr></table><p>These are starting roles, not rigid class assignments. An attacker with important debuffs may want some Focus, while a Supporter’s recovery might depend on HP rather than ATK. Role conversion also changes the result: Defender and Supporter Vitality gains use a ×1.1 role factor. For numerical examples and the full conversion table, read the <a href="#/guide/journey">Journey guide</a>. Do not maximize a stat simply because a high-rarity card happens to have that tag.</p><h2>How do matching, Sensory and Super Sensory differ?</h2><p>Character-name matching grants the separate +5% Training Effect bonus. Training-tag matching makes Sensory eligible, after which the game checks its chance. Super Sensory requires that sensory session and enough bond for the participating card. Neither rarity nor matching alone guarantees it. Bond can come from the card’s Journey Start effect, training and events. Unique effects may have their own threshold and action condition, so activating a unique does not automatically mean every session becomes Super Sensory. Inspect <a href="data/arcana.json">stored effects and unique descriptions</a> for the precise wording.</p><div class="callout warn">Community reroll priorities differ and come from different periods. None of them overrides the card’s level, conditions or your missing combat role.</div><h2>Which stored top cards fit each stat?</h2><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/arcana/ARCANA_INDEPENDENT_DRAGON_SSR_01_S.webp" alt=""><div><h3>No Pain, No Gain [Strength]</h3><p>A Strength package with a useful economy branch, this card is aimed at runs that can repeatedly train an ATK-scaling leader. Stored effects include +6% training effectiveness from level 1, starting bond +8 at level 5, and a sensory-chance value of +200 at level 10. Its unique rewards support quests after 60 bond rather than directly multiplying an attack. That distinction matters: a strong overall run card can help through resources even when its unique contributes no immediate combat damage.</p><p><b>Royalty Income:</b> After 60 bond, support quests receive a further 3% coin-gain bonus.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Supports a deliberate Strength training plan.</li><li>Unique effect has an explicit bond requirement.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Unique benefit requires its listed condition.</li><li>A stored S tier does not replace checking card level and leader scaling.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Stored Arcana tier:</b> S; conditional on the run.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/arcana/ARCANA_BUNNY_PRAY_SSR_01_S.webp" alt=""><div><h3>The Indomitable Masterpiece [Strength]</h3><p>This Strength choice combines general training improvement with small flat additions unlocked later. The local rows include +9% training effectiveness from level 1, an extra one PP during training at level 10, and an extra one Strength at level 30. Its unique has a 40-bond sensory condition, so it offers a different package from No Pain, No Gain’s quest economy. Compare actual unlocked effects before choosing between them; a low-level copy does not automatically provide every listed training addition.</p><p><b>Effective Marketing:</b> Its 40-bond sensory condition contributes 140 more sensory-chance value.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Supports a deliberate Strength training plan.</li><li>Unique effect has an explicit bond requirement.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Unique benefit requires its listed condition.</li><li>A stored S tier does not replace checking card level and leader scaling.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Stored Arcana tier:</b> S; conditional on the run.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/arcana/ARCANA_KINGDOM_KNIGHT_SSR_01_S.webp" alt=""><div><h3>A Knight&#x27;s Oath [Vitality]</h3><p>This Vitality option combines HP-oriented training with a sensory-chance unique that becomes available at 30 bond. The local rows list training effectiveness at +11% from level 1 and a starting +8 bond effect unlocked at level 5; these are not the live table’s maximum-level totals. It suits a protector or HP-scaling healer whose run repeatedly trains Vitality. Its unique helps sensory frequency rather than granting a guaranteed sensory outcome, so compare the whole training package with your available alternatives.</p><p><b>The Grand Master&#x27;s Prestige:</b> At 30 bond, its sensory condition adds 180 to the sensory-chance value.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Supports a deliberate Vitality training plan.</li><li>Unique effect has an explicit bond requirement.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Unique benefit requires its listed condition.</li><li>A stored S tier does not replace checking card level and leader scaling.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Stored Arcana tier:</b> S; conditional on the run.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/arcana/ARCANA_DRESS_STRANIS_SSR_01_S.webp" alt=""><div><h3>Young Lady of Stranis [Vitality]</h3><p>This card emphasizes repeated Vitality sessions and a relatively early 30-bond unique. The stored level-1 training-effectiveness row is +10%, while the level-10 training-chance row gives +4%; those effects have different jobs. It also unlocks a starting +9 bond effect at level 20. Choose it when reliable HP-oriented development supports the leader’s actual kit. The unique’s flat Vitality gain rewards repeated training, but it does not replace protection against a boss that defeats the party through control or burst.</p><p><b>One Step Further:</b> Reaching 30 bond enables an extra one Vitality on training.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Supports a deliberate Vitality training plan.</li><li>Unique effect has an explicit bond requirement.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Unique benefit requires its listed condition.</li><li>A stored S tier does not replace checking card level and leader scaling.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Stored Arcana tier:</b> S; conditional on the run.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/arcana/ARCANA_COUNTERSIDE_HILDE_SSR_01_S.webp" alt=""><div><h3>Nostalgia Strikes Back [Endurance]</h3><p>Use this Endurance option when repeated defensive training supports the leader’s job. The stored base row gives +7% training effectiveness from level 1, and level 10 unlocks both starting PP +6 and a +4% condition-effect row. Its unique needs 60 bond before adding flat Endurance to training. The starting bond row is only unlocked at level 35 in the local data, so an early copy may reach useful conditions more slowly than its fully unlocked version. It cannot compensate for an otherwise incompatible damage plan.</p><p><b>Bearing of a Commander:</b> Training gains one additional Endurance once bond reaches 60.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Supports a deliberate Endurance training plan.</li><li>Unique effect has an explicit bond requirement.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Unique benefit requires its listed condition.</li><li>A stored S tier does not replace checking card level and leader scaling.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Stored Arcana tier:</b> S; conditional on the run.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/arcana/ARCANA_CONSTRUCT_BOMB_SSR_01_S.webp" alt=""><div><h3>Made by Petra♡ [Focus]</h3><p>This Focus card supports debuff-dependent leaders rather than every offensive build. Its stored level-1 training effectiveness is +8%; level 10 unlocks starting Focus +4 and bond +7. The sensory-chance unique waits for 60 bond, so plan the early run without assuming that later bonus is already active. Focus improves Effect Hit conversion, while immunity and guaranteed resistance remain separate constraints. Check that the leader’s important debuff can affect the target before sacrificing direct damage or survival for a specialized accuracy package.</p><p><b>An Entrepreneur&#x27;s Network:</b> At 60 bond, sensory sessions gain a further 130 sensory-chance value.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Supports a deliberate Focus training plan.</li><li>Unique effect has an explicit bond requirement.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Unique benefit requires its listed condition.</li><li>A stored S tier does not replace checking card level and leader scaling.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Stored Arcana tier:</b> S; conditional on the run.</p></div></div><div class="g-card"><img loading="lazy" width="72" height="72" src="images/arcana/ARCANA_WEST_LANCER_SSR_01_S.webp" alt=""><div><h3>A Satisfying Meal [Protection]</h3><p>This Protection card is useful when resisting relevant enemy effects is a real requirement. Its stored training-effectiveness row begins at +6%, with an additional one Protection unlocked at level 25. Its economy unique waits for 60 bond and benefits support quests, which is separate from the leader’s final Effect RES. Starting bond +9 is a level-20 unlock in the local data. Compare unlocked effects with your actual copy: neither the stat tag nor a high stored tier grants immunity to every encounter mechanic.</p><p><b>Model Member:</b> With 60 bond, support quests gain another 5% in coins.</p><div class="g-proscons"><div class="pros"><h4>Pros</h4><ul><li>Supports a deliberate Protection training plan.</li><li>Unique effect has an explicit bond requirement.</li></ul></div><div class="cons"><h4>Cons</h4><ul><li>Unique benefit requires its listed condition.</li><li>A stored S tier does not replace checking card level and leader scaling.</li></ul></div></div><p class="g-rating"><b>Stored Arcana tier:</b> S; conditional on the run.</p></div></div><h2>FAQ</h2><div class="g-faq"><details><summary>Does a matching savior guarantee Sensory?</summary><p>No. Character matching and training-tag matching are different checks.</p></details><details><summary>Does bond carry between runs?</summary><p>The new run starts from its configured bond and Arcana starting effects; do not assume the previous run’s accumulated bond persists.</p></details><details><summary>Can Focus bypass immunity?</summary><p>No. Effect Hit helps the resistance check; an immune target remains a different restriction.</p></details><details><summary>Why does the live table show higher values?</summary><p>Compare equal card levels and effect unlocks. This guide identifies the stored local data rather than claiming a max-level live total.</p></details></div><h2>What to read next</h2><ul><li><a href="#/arcana">Card database</a>.</li><li><a href="#/guide/journey">Journey planning</a>.</li><li><a href="#/tiers">Tier and investment context</a>.</li></ul>',
	},
	"new-gille": {
		group: "new",
		title: "Gille Review: break support and best teams",
		sub: "What Gille does, her abilities, which stats to pick and the best teams with swap options.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		review: "apostle-gille",
		html: "<img class=\"g-hero\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/journey_bg/ILLUST_BG_JOURNEY_APOSTLE_GILLE_01.webp\" alt=\"\"><p class=\"lead\">Gille is a Chaos caster who turns your team's Toughness breaks into extra damage. She is great with Shatter users like Witch of Favoritism Asherah and Tyria, and weak without them.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><b>Shatter in one line:</b> Shatter is a buff that adds Toughness damage. Only Gille, Tyria and Witch Asherah can give it, and Gille's ultimate only rewards allies who already have it.</div>",
	},
	"new-witch-asherah": {
		group: "new",
		title: "Witch of Favoritism Asherah Review: boss breaker and best teams",
		sub: "Her break loop, abilities, stats to pick and the best teams with swap options.",
		updated: "13/11/2026",
		review: "dark-unknown",
		html: "<img class=\"g-hero\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"images/journey_bg/ILLUST_BG_JOURNEY_DARK_UNKNOWN_01.webp\" alt=\"\"><p class=\"lead\">Witch of Favoritism Asherah is an Order striker made to break bosses. Under her own Shatter, Phantom Sever adds 5 Toughness damage to a boss, and every hit on a broken boss resets it.</p><div class=\"callout tip\"><b>The loop:</b> cast Lucula Noctis for Shatter, then Phantom Sever to break, then hit the broken boss to reset Phantom Sever and gain chain damage.</div>",
	},
};
